// Renovate — THE update lane for this repository, version updates and security
// alike (CLOUD-655, CLOUD-660). There is no second bot: `.github/dependabot.yml`
// is deleted, and `ci-local-parity` refuses a tree where it comes back.
//
// JSON5 rather than JSON so the reasoning lives beside the keys it explains. The
// keys here are not preferences: five of them are the whole CI-cost-and-coverage
// mechanism and a sixth is what lets the lane's commits land at all, and `mise
// run ci-local-parity` refuses this file if any is dropped or moved somewhere a
// preset outranks.
//
// WHY THIS BOT AND NOT THE OTHER. Dependabot's `package-ecosystem` values are a
// fixed published list with no `mise`, no asdf `.tool-versions` and no
// `rust-toolchain`, so `.github/dependabot.yml` could not cover this repo's tool
// table at any setting. That is a capability gap, not a config omission, and its
// cost was measured: `[tools] rust` sat at 1.85.0 against a stable of 1.97.1 —
// twelve releases — freezing two crates and rejecting a third, with `mise run
// lock-check` green throughout because it asks a different currency question
// (have the artifacts for the versions we ALREADY pinned changed).
//
// ONE UPDATER PER MANIFEST IS NOT A STYLE RULE, and with one bot left it is
// simply "every ecosystem this repo maintains is named below". `ci-local-parity`
// decides that rather than trusting the comment: an ecosystem this repo carries
// and this file does not name fails the gate.
//
// THE LANE IS LIVE, AND HAS BEEN SINCE 2026-08-18. It opened #493 (landed) and
// #503, so nothing below is prospective. What it could not do until CLOUD-692
// was get anything landed: every head it proposes is a draft, and no workflow
// was scoped to `renovate/**`. `.github/workflows/auto-bot-land.yml` is that
// workflow, and this file's keys are written to compose with it.
{
  $schema: "https://docs.renovatebot.com/renovate-schema.json",

  extends: ["config:recommended"],

  // THE SCOPE FENCE, and it is load-bearing rather than tidy. `config:recommended`
  // enables every manager Renovate ships with; `enabledManagers` is an allowlist,
  // so what is named here is the whole of what this bot touches. With the second
  // bot retired the fence changed meaning rather than lapsing: it used to keep
  // two updaters off one manifest, and it now decides whether any manifest is
  // updated at all. `ci-local-parity`'s property 14 reads this list against the
  // ecosystems this repo maintains, so a name dropped here goes unmaintained
  // loudly rather than silently.
  //
  // Each name arrived in the SAME commit that took the ecosystem out of
  // `.github/dependabot.yml`'s version-update lane, so no revision of the tree
  // had one covered twice or not at all: `github-actions` in CLOUD-657, which
  // went first for touching no manifest that encodes the MSRV floor, and `cargo`
  // in CLOUD-658, which is the lane that does.
  enabledManagers: ["mise", "github-actions", "cargo"],

  // THE THIRD COPY OF THE FLOOR, AND IT IS GATED (CLOUD-658). Dependabot's cargo
  // updater reads `rust-version` from the manifest natively; Renovate's does not
  // (`renovatebot/renovate#26314`, open, no implementation), so the number has to
  // be written here by hand for MSRV-aware resolution to survive the handover.
  //
  // A copy is not the defect — an UNGATED copy is. CLOUD-593 already put the
  // floor in two files and shipped `msrv-pin-agreement` to hold them equal, so
  // this is one more path in the same check rather than a new hazard:
  // `mise.toml [tools] rust` is the authority, and `Cargo.toml`'s `rust-version`
  // and this value are both derived copies that must agree with it. Major.minor,
  // because that is what `rust-version` carries.
  //
  // Raise the pin, then run `mise run msrv-pin-agreement`, which names every file
  // that disagrees.
  constraints: { rust: "1.98" },

  // --- the five keys `ci-local-parity` holds ---------------------------------
  //
  // Every `pull_request` workflow in this repo gates on `draft == false`, so a
  // draft head buys zero runner minutes. Dependabot's schema has no equivalent
  // knob, which is why its lane was measured at 13 `ci.yml` runs and zero skips
  // over eight days (CLOUD-596) — a full matrix per SHA it proposes. CLOUD-596
  // priced the workaround (a new workflow and a PAT-driven ready) and declined
  // it. This is one key.
  draftPR: true,

  // KEPT BOUNDED, BUT NO LONGER AT "never" (CLOUD-692). The clause that made
  // `never` right is the one above it: a force-push on a readied PR is a
  // `synchronize` and another matrix, and `main` advances constantly here. That
  // clause does not survive `draftPR: true`. Every `pull_request` workflow gates
  // on `draft == false`, so while the PR is a draft a rebase grades zero checks
  // and costs zero runner minutes — the same key that makes the head free makes
  // rebasing it free.
  //
  // What `never` cost instead was the landing: `main` moves under the draft, the
  // head goes BEHIND, and a fast-forward is arithmetically unavailable. #503 sat
  // in exactly that state, holding the single `prConcurrentLimit` slot against
  // seven queued updates, because nothing rebased it and nothing could land it.
  // So this key was buying an economy on a lane that had stopped functioning.
  //
  // A rebase that arrives mid-CI on a head `auto-bot-land.yml` has readied is
  // absorbed rather than paid for: `ci.yml`'s `concurrency: cancel-in-progress`
  // cancels the run the rebase superseded, and the next one grades the head that
  // can actually land.
  //
  // THE CLAUSE THAT USED TO END THIS COMMENT IS RETIRED RATHER THAN DROPPED, and
  // the key below is what replaces it. It read: freezing the branch instead
  // (Renovate's `stopUpdatingLabel`) "costs a matrix that cannot land AND a
  // second one after the unfreeze, so it is strictly worse." That arithmetic is
  // right for a head that is TRANSIENTLY red — two matrices beats one — and it
  // silently assumed the only alternative was freezing EVERY head. See below for
  // what it costs when the redness is not transient.
  rebaseWhen: "behind-base-branch",

  // THE TERMINATOR (CLOUD-1207), and what it terminates is a loop every other key
  // in this file was individually right about.
  //
  // `draftPR: true` makes a rebase free, `rebaseWhen` above keeps the head
  // landable, and `auto-bot-land.yml` readies a landable draft so exactly one
  // matrix is spent on the SHA about to land. Each holds. What none of them
  // covers is a head that is READY and red for a reason no bump can fix: nothing
  // returns it to draft, so `main` advancing rebases a Ready PR, and each rebase
  // is a `synchronize` — a full matrix that cannot land, forever.
  //
  // MEASURED ON #676: seven days open, **372 workflow runs on one branch**, 8
  // rebase cycles on 2026-08-30 alone, each buying `CI` (~19 required checks),
  // `Rust`, `commit-lint` and `zizmor`. It was red on `commit-lint` — a claim a
  // bot commit structurally cannot make, which CLOUD-1207 fixes one file over —
  // and on `sbom-check`, whose licence table only a human regenerates
  // (CLOUD-1213). Neither is transient, and that is the case the retired clause
  // above did not price.
  //
  // WHY A LABEL AND NOT A SHA-KEYED RETRY, because the obvious fix does not work
  // and the reasoning is worth keeping: re-drafting a red head stops CI on THAT
  // sha, then the next `main` advance rebases, mints a fresh sha nothing has
  // graded, and the lander correctly readies it — one matrix per `main` advance,
  // the identical bill. A verdict keyed to a sha cannot bound a loop whose every
  // lap mints a new one. The label is keyed to the PULL REQUEST, so it survives
  // every rebase, and it works by stopping the rebases themselves: Renovate
  // leaves a labelled PR alone, so no new head is minted and no matrix is spent.
  //
  // THE COST, STATED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED. A frozen head goes stale and cannot
  // fast-forward, and it still holds the `prConcurrentLimit: 1` slot below. That
  // is the honest shape of the situation: a bot bump that needs a human is not
  // making progress whether or not this repository pays CI for it. Removing the
  // label is the resume, and the next rebase mints a head the lane readies once.
  stopUpdatingLabel: "renovate-frozen",

  // ONE HEAD IN FLIGHT. With `rebaseWhen` no longer `never` this is no longer the
  // second half of a pairing — it is the throttle in its own right, and it is
  // what makes the lane's cost legible: at most one draft accumulating, at most
  // one matrix in flight, and a queue that drains in landing order rather than a
  // fan of siblings proposing overlapping groups.
  //
  // It throttles; it does NOT wedge, which was the open question and is now
  // measured (CLOUD-692): #493 merged at 06:49:36Z and the released slot became
  // #503 at 06:50:56Z, 80 seconds later. So the cost of holding it at 1 is
  // latency on the tail of the queue, not a stuck queue — and the lander is what
  // keeps that latency bounded.
  prConcurrentLimit: 1,

  // Renovate's spelling of the 7-day cooldown CLOUD-105 bought on the Dependabot
  // lanes: let a churny release settle before a PR opens.
  minimumReleaseAge: "7 days",

  // THE SECURITY LANE, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE THIS REPOSITORY HAS (CLOUD-660,
  // CLOUD-688). `.github/dependabot.yml` retained a `cargo` entry so Dependabot
  // SECURITY updates would carry a subject `commit-lint` accepts; that lane
  // cannot fire. Measured 2026-08-19 against the live repository:
  //
  //     GET /repos/button-inc/batten/automated-security-fixes
  //       -> {"enabled": false, "paused": false}
  //     GET /repos/button-inc/batten/vulnerability-alerts   -> 204 (alerts on)
  //     GET /repos/button-inc/batten/dependabot/alerts?state=open -> 0
  //
  // So the shim asserted a lane the repository setting does not have (the
  // CLOUD-198 class), and deleting it withdraws no coverage. Renovate reads the
  // SAME advisory stream — the one that is on — which is why this block replaces
  // it rather than merely outliving it.
  //
  // `minimumReleaseAge: null` waives the seven-day cooldown above, and only
  // here: a cooldown is right for a churny feature release and wrong for a fix
  // to a known advisory. It is the one key in this file whose default would be
  // actively harmful rather than merely expensive.
  //
  // A security PR still opens as a draft, like every other head this lane
  // proposes. That is not a delay to landing: `auto-bot-land.yml` readies and
  // lands on its own tick, and the draft is what keeps a bump that will be
  // superseded from buying a matrix first.
  vulnerabilityAlerts: {
    enabled: true,
    minimumReleaseAge: null,
  },

  // --- and the key without which none of the above can ever land -------------
  //
  // Every commit here must satisfy `CONVENTIONAL_RE` (the `commit-lint` gate),
  // because PRs land by fast-forward. Renovate's default subject carries no type
  // prefix, so its PRs would fail commit-lint and could never land — the same
  // defect `.github/dependabot.yml`'s `commit-message.prefix` settings existed to
  // avoid before that file was deleted (CLOUD-660).
  //
  // THE TYPE CANNOT LIVE UP HERE, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN STYLISTIC
  // (CLOUD-676). `extends: ["config:recommended"]` above pulls in
  // `:semanticPrefixFixDepsChoreOthers`, whose FIRST rule is a catch-all:
  //
  //     { matchPackageNames: ["*"], semanticCommitType: "chore" }
  //
  // `packageRules` outrank top-level config, so a `semanticCommitType` written
  // here is set and then immediately overwritten for every package. Observed on
  // the lane's first run: every subject came out `chore(deps)` while this file
  // said `ci`, with every other key in it demonstrably in effect. The type
  // therefore lives in `packageRules` below, which are appended AFTER the
  // preset's and so win. `ci-local-parity` refuses a config that moves it back.
  //
  // `semanticCommitScope` is safe up here: no preset rule sets it.
  semanticCommits: "enabled",
  semanticCommitScope: "deps",

  packageRules: [
    {
      // The commit type, placed here rather than top-level for the reason above:
      // this rule is appended after `config:recommended`'s catch-all `chore` and
      // therefore beats it.
      //
      // `ci` for the two managers that describe how this repo BUILDS AND TESTS
      // itself rather than what it links against — the toolchain table and the
      // workflows.
      matchManagers: ["mise", "github-actions"],
      semanticCommitType: "ci",
    },
    {
      // `build` for cargo, and the difference is not cosmetic: this manager
      // moves `Cargo.toml` and `Cargo.lock`, which is the crate's own dependency
      // graph. It is also what the deleted security shim emitted
      // (`commit-message.prefix: "build(deps)"`), so a cargo subject did not
      // change type across the handover — worth keeping now that
      // `vulnerabilityAlerts` above carries that lane, since a security bump
      // reading differently from a version bump would be read as a signal.
      matchManagers: ["cargo"],
      semanticCommitType: "build",
      // What `.github/dependabot.yml`'s `groups: cargo: patterns: ["*"]` bought
      // (CLOUD-658). CLOUD-344's finding travels with it and is not repaired by
      // the move: a grouped batch is all-or-nothing, so one member that will not
      // build holds the whole set. What changes is where that is discovered — on
      // a draft PR that bought no matrix, rather than on a Ready head that
      // bought one.
      groupName: "cargo",
    },
    {
      // What `groups: actions: patterns: ["*"]` bought on this lane, kept across
      // the handover (CLOUD-657): a week's action bumps arrive as ONE pull
      // request. It matters more here than it did there, because
      // `prConcurrentLimit: 1` above means ungrouped updates would queue one at
      // a time behind each other rather than merely being noisy.
      //
      // This rule sets only `groupName`, so the `ci` above survives it.
      matchManagers: ["github-actions"],
      groupName: "actions",
    },
    {
      // A CEILING AGAINST A STRAY TAG, and the shape is `mise-action-floor`'s
      // (CLOUD-404) rather than a preference: a denylist against a silent
      // DOWNGRADE this lane would otherwise land with no human in the loop.
      //
      // Measured 2026-08-31 against the upstream repository. `regal`'s newest
      // RELEASE is `v0.42.0` — commit `bcdaa1dd4125`, 2026-07-16, module
      // `github.com/open-policy-agent/regal`, `go 1.26`. Its TAG list also
      // carries `v2.2.2` and `v2.2.3`:
      //
      //   v2.2.3  ecf4652a4158  2024-11-25  "Testing versioning"
      //   v2.2.2  34ba147b59e6  2024-11-25  a codeql-action bump
      //
      // Both sit on `github.com/anderseknert/regal` at `go 1.22.5` — the module
      // path from before the project was donated to open-policy-agent, roughly
      // twenty months OLDER than v0.42.0. They are the debris of somebody
      // experimenting with Go's `/vN` module suffix, and the commit subject says
      // so in as many words.
      //
      // Renovate's datasource reads TAGS, so semver ranks `2.2.3` above `0.42.0`
      // and #494 proposed it as an upgrade. It is a regression to an abandoned
      // module path.
      //
      // WHAT MAKES IT A HAZARD RATHER THAN NOISE. `auto-bot-land.yml` lands a
      // bot bump on green CI with nobody in the loop, and this repository's Rego
      // corpus is linted by regal — a twenty-month-older linter would quietly
      // stop enforcing whatever it had not yet learned. The only thing standing
      // between the proposal and a landing today is that those tags carry no
      // release assets, so `mise` cannot resolve them and the install fails.
      // That is luck, not a gate: a release cut against either tag makes the
      // downgrade installable and this lane would take it.
      //
      // The ceiling is `<1.0.0` rather than a pin, so every real 0.x release
      // still arrives on its own. Remove it if regal genuinely ships a 1.x or
      // 2.x from the current module path — the tell is a RELEASE, with assets,
      // whose `go.mod` reads `github.com/open-policy-agent/regal`.
      // Spelled as "no major for this package" rather than as an
      // `allowedVersions` ceiling, and that is not a dodge around `cap-drift` —
      // it is the claim being made. `cap-drift` pairs an `allowedVersions` rule
      // with a cap in `Cargo.toml` (CLOUD-593), because for a CRATE a bot
      // withholding a version the manifest admits is a silent stall. regal is a
      // `[tools]` entry with no manifest cap to pair with, so that rule would
      // read as the half-lift it exists to catch. What this row asserts is
      // narrower and true: regal's major line is spurious, so do not propose one.
      matchManagers: ["mise"],
      matchPackageNames: ["aqua:open-policy-agent/regal"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      enabled: false,
    },
    {
      // STOP PROPOSING RENOVATE 44 UNTIL THE SEAM IS RENAMED (CLOUD-1262).
      //
      // Renovate 44 reads the `RENOVATE_CONFIG` environment variable as INLINE
      // JSON5 config. `mise-tasks/renovate-config-validator.sh:32` uses that same
      // name for the path seam its suite sets, so the validator is handed
      // `/tmp/bats-run-.../renovate.json5` as config CONTENT and dies at
      // `JSON5: invalid character 't' at 1:2` — the `t` of `/tmp`. Caught by
      // `tests/renovate-config-validator.bats:37`, which is the gate doing its job.
      //
      // THE MEASURED REASON THIS IS A RULE AND NOT A COMMENT. That collision was
      // already known, already explained directly above `mise.toml`'s own pin
      // ("THE PIN STAYS AT 41.173.1, AND THAT IS A FINDING RATHER THAN AN
      // OMISSION"), and already recorded in `ec2c9c15`'s commit message. None of
      // that stopped the bump being made and reverted TWICE:
      //
      //   lap 1  44.50.3, `verify` refused, reverted in ec2c9c15
      //   lap 2  44.41.0 via GitHub #494, `verify` refused identically, reverted
      //
      // Both laps cost a full cold `verify` to rediscover a settled decision, and
      // lap 2's author had the comment on screen. Prose is feedforward; this is
      // the mechanism. Renovate reads neither a gate nor a comment, so it
      // re-proposes 44 indefinitely, and `auto-bot-land.yml` merges a bot bump on
      // green CI with nobody in the loop — the only thing stopping lap 3 landing
      // is that `verify` happens to be red for it, which is luck rather than a
      // refusal.
      //
      // THIS IS NOT THE FIX AND MUST NOT BE MISTAKEN FOR ONE. Renaming the seam
      // means editing the task AND its suite, both frozen by
      // `shell edit refused` whose sole route is `rule read first`. CLOUD-1262
      // tracks that retirement. This row buys quiet, not currency: the tool whose
      // whole job is dependency currency stays three majors stale until the port
      // lands, and leaving this row here while forgetting CLOUD-1262 is exactly
      // how that staleness becomes permanent. DELETE THIS ROW as part of the port.
      matchManagers: ["mise"],
      matchPackageNames: ["npm:renovate"],
      matchUpdateTypes: ["major"],
      enabled: false,
    },
  ],
}
